# A `return` at the MAIN program's top level — the ACCEPT side of ouroboros#146.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that no fixture in the suite had a top-level
# `return` at all. When #146 refused a MODULE's top-level return, the reason it
# is safe to keep the program's is that a program-level `return` really does
# mean "end the program, exit 0, value discarded" — which is exactly what the
# emitted C `return 0` in main does. That claim had zero coverage, so the
# refusal could have been widened to cover this too and nothing would have
# noticed the loss.
#
# Exit code is part of the contract here, not just stdout: the VM discards the
# returned value and exits 0.

print of "start"

n is 0

# a NOT-taken conditional return: an early exit that fires here would truncate
# everything below, so this pins the branch as well as the return
if n > 5:
    print of "unreachable-1"
    return 99

# a `return` inside a loop body that is not reached, for the same reason
for i in range of 3:
    if i > 10:
        return 7
    print of i

loop while n < 2:
    n is n + 1
print of n

# The taken one. Three things are pinned here at once: everything after it must
# NOT run; the exit code is 0 even though the returned value is 42; and the
# returned EXPRESSION is still evaluated for its side effects before being
# discarded, so `emitted` prints. A fix that simply dropped the value would
# pass the exit-code check and lose the side effect.
define emit_then(v) as:
    print of "emitted"
    return v

if n == 2:
    print of "returning"
    return (emit_then of 42)

print of "unreachable-2"
